      "description": "Create one Pro Flash pixel-art image with an optional left-box style image.\n\nCustom sizes (Beta) accept width and height from 16 to 256 in multiples of 4 pixels.\nQuery get_pro_flash_capabilities for native presets.\nThe style image accepts {url}, {base64}, or {source_image_id}; prefer URLs\nfor uploaded images. Style art must fit the native canvas without resizing.\nFor several assets of one game, pass the same anchor sprite as style_image on\nevery call (e.g. the hero's south rotation URL from get_character); without it\neach call picks its own look.\nstyle_options selects color_palette, outline, detail and shading independently.\nDefault style uses existing PixelLab art. For a console or palette look (1-bit,\nGame Boy, PICO-8, any palette from Lospec) pass `palette` as its hex colours plus\n`palette_name`: the image is drawn for the palette and every pixel of the result\nis one of its colours; the style image keeps lending its look. Returns one image.\nFailed pixel grids use image-to-pixel-art with fixer on the generated box.\nPoll get_image(job_id); use source_image_id after completion.\n\nCost: by LONGEST SIDE — 5 generations up to 96px, 6 from 100 to 208, 9 from\n212 to 256, for one image. A non-square canvas goes by its longer side, so\n128x72 is 6 and 32x96 is 5. This is the whole Pro Flash ladder (create, edit\nand inpaint charge the same), and it is NOT the Pro tools' 10/15/25 tier.\nNo parameter caps a charge, and the quote is labelled provisional rather\nthan a contractual cap — but the ladder above is what actually gets billed,\nso treat those figures as the price.\nget_pro_flash_capabilities(operation=, width=, height=) prices an exact size\nfor free before you spend.\n\nPixelLab AI-powered pixel art generation service.\n\nUse of this service is subject to PixelLab's Terms of Service:\nhttps://pixellab.ai/termsofservice\n\nThis MCP server provides programmatic access to generate:\n- Characters with multiple directional views\n- Character animations\n- Isometric tiles\n- Top-down tilesets for game maps\n- Sidescroller tilesets for platformer games\n\nAll generation operations are non-blocking and return job IDs for status tracking.\nRequires PixelLab subscription (with credit fallback support).\n\n**Pixel tools (free, exact, you are the artist)**: one command line, pixelart_workbench(argv=[...]), inspects, edits, draws and animates pixel art by your own decisions; combine it with the AI tools above. Start with pixelart_workbench(argv=['describe', 'start'], mode='low'|'high') — low acts directly with few tokens, high plans out loud and reviews every frame. Animated effects (spells, hits, explosions, auras): vfx_workbench(argv=['describe', 'start']) — same ids, exports to the user's My effects.\n\n**Git access**: Clone and push game project source code with standard git.\nUse `list_projects` to see your projects and their git clone URLs.\n\nexec tool declaration:\n```ts\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__pixellab__create_image_pro_flash(args: {\n  description: string;\n  height?: number;\n  // At most this many colours in the image (2-64). With a palette, they are picked from it.\n  max_colors?: number | null;\n  no_background?: boolean;\n  // Target palette, 2 to 256 hex colours as a JSON array, e.g. [\"#081820\", \"#346856\", \"#88c070\", \"#e0f8d0\"] for Game Boy or [\"#000000\", \"#ffffff\"] for 1-bit. The image is drawn for these colours and every pixel of the result is one of them.\n  palette?: Array<string> | null;\n  // Optional name of the palette (e.g. 'Game Boy', 'PICO-8'); naming it helps the look.\n  palette_name?: string | null;\n  project_id?: string | null;\n  seed?: number;\n  // Style anchor. Exactly ONE of its three sources: `url` also accepts an `upload:<id>` from get_upload_url or a data: URL, and `source_image_id` is an image you own (what a Pro Flash job returns). It must fit INSIDE the output canvas both ways — a larger one is refused, so crop it; resizing pixel art is never the answer. Reuse one anchor across calls to keep a set consistent.\n  style_image?: {\n  base64?: string | null;\n  source_image_id?: string | null;\n  url?: string | null;\n  // How this reference should guide appearance.\n  usage_description?: string | null;\n} | null;\n  // Choose which visual traits to copy from the style image.\n  style_options?: { color_palette?: boolean; detail?: boolean; outline?: boolean; shading?: boolean; } | null;\n  // Native sizes: 16x16 (experimental), 24x24, 32x32, 32x48, 48x48, 64x64, 96x64, 96x96, 128x128. Custom size (Beta): integer width 16–256 and height 16–256. Both dimensions must be multiples of 4 pixels. Pixel-grid recovery uses image-to-pixel-art with fixer when needed. No native image resizing.\n  width?: number;\n}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\n```"
